@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] bench-resources-labeled
Every billable GCP resource a run creates (cluster, node VMs, disks, addresses) MUST carry the
labels `dfs-bench=true` and `dfs-bench-run=<run_id>`, or have the run's cluster name `dfsb-<run_id>`
in its name, tags or description. `bin/leak-check` and `bin/sweep` find leftovers only this way.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] down-proves-clean
`bin/down <run_id>` MUST exit non-zero if any resource of that run, including E2B sandboxes,
still exists after teardown. Kubernetes objects that own cloud resources (PVCs, LoadBalancer
services) MUST be deleted before the cluster is destroyed.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:security] sweep-only-bench-resources
Cleanup scripts MUST select resources by the `dfs-bench` labels, sandbox metadata or the exact
`dfsb-<run_id>` cluster name, never by broader name patterns: dust-dev hosts other people's
experiments.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:performance] sandbox-ttl
Bench E2B sandboxes MUST be created with metadata `dfs-bench=true` and `dfs-bench-run=<run_id>`
and a timeout of at most 15 minutes, extended only while the orchestrator is alive. None may live
longer than 3 hours: our sandbox reaper does not manage them.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:security] isolated-credentials
Scripts MUST act only as the dust-dev service account, through the gcloud config and per-run
kubeconfig under `.state/`. They MUST NOT read or modify the user's own gcloud config,
application default credentials or kubeconfig, which hold production access.

@cc [owner:fontanierh,label:testing] comparable-results
Every result file MUST record the scenario, the corpus (kind, and for jd its manifest sha256), each
mount's freshness budget and the mount client's memory limit. Rounds MUST only be compared when
these match: the scatter corpus and jd's corpus stress a mount differently.
